The code statements that lead to the bug are:
mutex_unlock(&oa->metrics_lock);
drm_dbg(&oa->xe->drm, "Added config %s id=%i\n", oa_config->uuid, oa_config->id);
return oa_config->id;
Reason:
After releasing `oa->metrics_lock`, the `oa_config` pointer is no longer protected. A concurrent call to `xe_oa_remove_config_ioctl()` can guess the id and free `oa_config`, leading to a use-after-free when `oa_config->uuid` and `oa_config->id` are accessed.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
id = oa_config->id;
drm_dbg(&oa->xe->drm, "Added config %s id=%i\n", oa_config->uuid, id);
mutex_unlock(&oa->metrics_lock);
return id;
Reason:
By caching `oa_config->id` into the local variable `id` while the lock is still held, all accesses to `oa_config` (including `oa_config->uuid`) happen before the lock is released. After unlocking, only the local `id` is used, eliminating the race condition where `oa_config` could be freed by another thread.

